मक वह वानर वृक्षों पर चढ़ कर एक शाखा से दूसरी शाखा",
      "textEn": "476\n\nEvam—in this way; Deshan—to various states; Viprakurvan—troubling; Dushayan—contaminating; Cha—and; Kula—aristocratic;\nStriah—to women; Shrutva—by listening; Su-lalitam—extremely sweet; Gita—song; Girim—in the mountain; Raivatakam—Raivatak\nNamak; Yayau—went. Once, when Dvivid was engaged in harassing neighboring states and defiling noble women,\nhe heard a very sweet singing sound coming from the Rewatk mountain.\nHe heard it. Therefore, he reached there. There, seeing Rama, the beautiful one, the one with the lotus garland. The beautiful one, with all her limbs, is in the middle. 9>. Having drunk the Varuna's nectar, my eyes are filled with tears.\nHe who is moving, is different from the body, like a warrior. Meaning of the word\n\nTatra—there; Apashyat—seen; Yadu-patim—the lord of the Yadus; Ramam—to Ram; Pushkar—of the lotus; Malinam—garlands;\nWearing; Su-darshaniya—extremely attractive; Sarva—all; Angam—of the limbs; Lalana—of the young women; Yuth—of the group; Mad—intoxicated; Vihvala—restless; Lochanam—to the eyes; Vibhrajamanam—beautiful, shining; Vapusha—of one's body; Prabhinnam—arouched; Iva—like; Varanam—an elephant;\nHaathi—elephant. There he saw Shri Balram, the lord of the Yadus, adorned with a garland of lotus flowers,\nand every part of whom looked extremely attractive. They were singing among the young women and\nbecause they had drunk Varuni liquor, therefore their eyes were moving in such a way as if they were intoxicated. His body was gleaming and he was behaving like a raging elephant. The wicked one is like a deer in the branches, a deer in the boughs, a deer in the wind.\nThe wheel, the sound of the anklet, shows itself. 11. Meaning of the word\n\nDushtah - wicked; Shakha-mrigah - monkey (a tree-dwelling animal); Shakham - on the branch; Aarudha - mounted;\nKampayant - shaking; Druman - trees; Chakre - done; Kilakila-shabdam - the sound of kilakila (a type of bird);\nAtmanam - oneself; Sampradarshayan - showing. That wicked monkey climbed onto the branch of the tree and, shaking the trees and making cries,\nstarted showing his presence. Meaning: The word 'Shakhamrug' indicates that the two-horned one used to climb trees like ordinary monkeys. Srila Prabhupada writes, \"That monkey named Dvivida climbed the trees, from one branch to another.\""
