      "textEn": "478\n\nTam—on him, on the two-armed one; Gravna—stone; Praharat—threw; Kruddha—angry; Balah—Balarama; Praharatam—those who throw weapons; Varaha—the best; Sah—he, the two-armed one; Vanchayitva—saving; Graavanam—stone; Madira—alcohol; Kalasham—\npot; Kapi—monkey; Grihitva—capturing; Hailayam aas—jokingly; Dhrutah—cunning—Cunning; Tam—to him, Balarama;\nKopayan—angry; Hasan—laughing; Nirbhidya—breaking; Kalasham—the vessel; Dushtah—wicked; Vasansi—clothes (of the young women); Asphalayat—pulled; Balam—to Balarama; Kadarthi-kritya—disrespectfully; Bal-van—powerful; Vipra-chakre—\ninsulted; Mad—with false ego; Uddhata—swollen; Uddhata—puffed up. The best warrior, Balram, angrily threw a stone at him, but that cunning monkey\nsaved himself from that stone and blew away Balram's wine container. The wicked Dvivida,\nwhile laughing at Balram, made him even angrier and broke that vessel, and by pulling the clothes of the young women,\nhe further humiliated Balram.\nIn this way, with false pride,\nBlooming and becoming a pot, the mighty monkey continued to insult Shri Balram. Having seen their humility, the countries were disturbed. Angry, Mushalamadatta, struck, four times. 16. Meaning of the word\n\nTam—that; Tasya—his; Avinayam—stubbornness; Dristva—by seeing; Deshan—to the kingdoms; Cha—and; Tat—by him;\nUpadrutaan—agitated; Krodhah—angry; Mushalam—his mace; Adatta—accepted; Halam—his plow; Cha—and;\nAri—enemy; Jighansaya—with the desire to kill. Lord Balarama saw the stubborn behavior of that monkey and considered the destruction-leela (mischief) caused by him in the surrounding kingdoms. In this way, Balram, being angry, decided to end his\nenemy and picked up his mace and his plow. Meaning: Avinayam means shameless.\" Dwivid, devoid of humility and modesty,\ncommitted extremely wicked deeds shamelessly.\nDvivida, in front of Balram, the shameless behavior that he displayed,\nin addition to that, Balram was aware of the great disturbances caused by him towards the common people. Therefore, the criminal monkey will now have to die. Even the wise, with great valor, raising their hands. By the power of the unconcealed light, it was struck by the strength of the head. 17. Meaning of the word\n\nDvividah - Dvivida; Api - also; Maha - great; Veeryah - strong; Shalam - to the Sal tree; Udyamya - lifting; Paniina - with one's own hands; Abhyetya - coming close; Tarasa - quickly; Tena - by him; Balam - to Balarama; Murdhani - on the head; Atadayat - struck. The mighty Dvivida also moved forward to wage war. Uprooting a tree of Shal with one hand"
